  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Cold front brings cooler temperatures alongside gusty westerly\n   winds, dry air, and elevated fire weather this afternoon.\n\n-  Ridge of high pressure rebuilds early this upcoming week with \n   a warming trend.\n\n-  Thunderstorms expand northward Tuesday and Wednesday afternoons.\n \n\nA few lingering showers and thunderstorms are expected this \nafternoon across northwest and southwest Montana, otherwise a cold\nfront will bring a much drier airmass to the region. Gusty\nafternoon winds will lead to elevate fire weather conditions,\nparticularly in southwest Montana. Skies clear this evening with \ngusty winds diminishing around sunset. \n\nHigh pressure ridging builds back in Monday lasting through \nWednesday with a gradual warming trend as high temperatures reach \n10 degrees above normal by Tuesday and Wednesday. After a few \nmorning showers on Tuesday, thunderstorm chances may expand \nnorthward to about the I-90 corridor in western Montana. Much will\ndepend on if morning cloud cover lingers into the afternoon and \nlimits instability and thus thunderstorm coverage. Monsoon \nmoisture spreads over most of the Northern Rockies on Wednesday \n(potentially Thursday as well), expanding areal coverage of \nshower/thunderstorm chances. This activity will likely be a mix \nof dry and wet thunderstorms, thus new fire starts will be a \nconcern.\n\nNearly all ensemble guidance supports a trough into the Pacific \nNorthwest Friday into Saturday knocking down the ridge. This \npattern supports increased afternoon winds and a slight cool down \nin daytime temperatures, thus fire weather concerns become \nelevated once again.",
